00:00:06.309 --> 00:00:08.150 align:start position:0%
european union environment ministers  have exchanged views on new rules to  monitor  report on and check carbon dioxide  emissions from maritime transport  the ministers weighed whether the  proposals to harmonise surveillance of  the main gas  responsible for global warming are  balanced and flexible enough  and do not impose a heavy administrative  burden  co2 emissions represent about 98  of the greenhouse gases emitted by ships  it's hoped that the eu model will serve  as an example  for the creation of a global system  the ministers also welcomed new  proposals to stop the spread of  introduced  animal and plant species into the eu  which are causing severe damage to the  environment  invasive alien species are transported  outside their normal habitat through  human action  this can hurt the new ecosystem and have  serious economic and social consequences  around twelve 000 alien species are  thought to be found in europe  about 10 to 15 percent of them have  spread and are costing the eu at least  12 billion euros  each year the new measures would involve  a prevention plan  an early warning and rapid response  system plus steps to manage the species  as they spread
 

